# Tier 4 (docs/tengoku-security-plan.md §3): maintainer plus a second approver.
# Either owner's approval counts: the maintainer's second account reviews what the maintainer's main account opens
# (GitHub never lets an author approve their own pull request).
# Tiers 1–2 (data/tentative, data/staging) have no owner on purpose: bot PRs must not need a human.
# THE DEFAULT (2026-10-10, after a review found tool and gate files that matched no rule): everything is owned by both accounts unless a line below says otherwise, so a new
# tool, script, config or root file needs a person without anyone remembering to list it. The exceptions are the content lane and the bot's data, further down.
*                        @mikael-bashir @mikaelbashir14096545
/Tengoku.lean            @mikael-bashir @mikaelbashir14096545
/Tengoku/                @mikael-bashir @mikaelbashir14096545   # the seed and the tree's own files; the content lane below takes the libraries out
# THE CONTENT LANE (user's decision 2026-10-10): the library folders and their umbrella files carry translations and proofs, and need no human owner; a library's
# FIRST part also adds its line to Tengoku/All.lean, which stays owned, so the first shard of every translation job is read by a person. Later parts (they
# change only the library's own folder, umbrella and data/intake) and the bot's staging records flow on the gates alone once the ruleset counts no plain approval.
# Registering a source, the seed, every tool, workflow, schema and script stay owned. Later lines win, so the lines that give ownership back come after these.
/Tengoku/*/
/Tengoku/*.lean
# Native is the tree's own novel content (class `native`, its own gate native_check.py): the user counts novel proofs as content, so it is in the lane on purpose.
/Tengoku/Native/
/Tengoku/Native.lean
/Tengoku/Seed/           @mikael-bashir @mikaelbashir14096545
/Tengoku/All.lean        @mikael-bashir @mikaelbashir14096545   # a library's first part adds its line here: a person reads it
